<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Qarinvand dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">550 CE – 839 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A branch of the great Parthian noble house of Karen that ruled the mountains of Tabaristan for three centuries, until its last prince Mazyar rebelled against the Abbasids.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-karen">Karen</a> were one of the seven great noble houses of Parthian and Sasanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-isfandiyar">Ibn Isfandiyar's</a> history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> granted the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukhra">Sukhra</a> land in the mountains of Tabaristan around 550, after their service against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalites</a>. The family held the highland district later called the 'Mountains of Qarin'.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Qarinvandids were local princes with the Persian title ispahbad or similar, ruling a Zoroastrian peasant society in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alborz">Alborz</a> forests and valleys. After the Arab conquest they held out alongside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dabuyid-dynasty">Dabuyid dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bavand-dynasty">Bavand dynasty</a>, paying tribute but keeping their faith. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandad-hormozd">Vandad Hormozd</a> led a revolt against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> in the 780s, and his son Qarin continued the family's power.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazyar">Mazyar ibn Qarin</a> converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>, but rebelled against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirid dynasty</a> in 839. He was captured and executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> in 840, ending the dynasty.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/qarinvand-dynasty.jpg" alt="Qarinvand dynasty" width="320" height="244"><figcaption>Image: Swordrist · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bavandids_1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>550 CE – 839 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vandad Hormozd, Qarin ibn Vandad, Mazyar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zoroastrianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Persian, Tabari</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qarinvandids, Qarinids</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=694&amp;focus=qarinvand-dynasty" aria-label="Qarinvand dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6076 998 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Qarinvand dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6396 1254h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6396 1254h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iran. <a href="/atlas?year=694&amp;focus=qarinvand-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

